clean_summary:
A gift from the Gods. Dealing with multiple models and many environments in the same RL codebase while respecting correctness constraints, such as avoiding train/inference tokenization mismatch, is becoming very difficult.

There is a draft PR that addresses this issue, but the author is glad they won’t have to maintain it now. The hope is that the field can standardize on one abstraction.

@PrimeIntellect: "Introducing Renderers. RL trainers work in tokens, while environments work in messages. Going back and forth corrupts sampled tokens, wasting compute on every agentic turn. With Renderers, we fix this mismatch. This unlocks over three times throughput on popular open models."

Taco Cohen argues that it is no longer tenable to believe that AI can only reproduce the training data, especially after models like o1 released in 2024. He emphasizes that recursive self-improvement has barely begun and that recent breakthroughs, such as solving the Erdos unit distance problem, show that AI can tackle long-standing mathematical challenges. Cohen suggests that claims about human activities forever beyond AI are just excuses, as the necessary technical, financial, and human resources are being deployed to solve these problems. He urges to assume artificial superintelligence is coming and to plan accordingly.

@wtgowers: "AI has now solved a major open problem — one of the best known Erdos problems called the unit distance problem, one that many mathematicians had tried."

The linked article reports that an OpenAI model has disproved a central conjecture in discrete geometry, marking a milestone in AI-driven mathematics.

